# Particle IDs conform to the 2017 revision of the
# Particle Data Group Monte Carlo Particle Numbering Scheme
# (see http://pdg.lbl.gov/2018/reviews/rpp2018-rev-monte-carlo-numbering.pdf)
#
# B(GT) values were provided in tabular form by T. Wakasa, et. al, Phys. Rev. C
# 85, 6 (2012). These values were given in terms of energy transfer with respect
# to the ground state of 208Pb. To correct for excitation energy with respect to
# the ground state of 208Bi, 2.367 MeV was subtracted from each energy bin. It is
# assumed these energies are the bin-centers of 1 MeV bins. The maximum energy
# transfer bin used was the provided value at 62.85 MeV energy transfer. Because
# this data was in 1 MeV bins, no care was taken to align provided energy values
# with excitations in 208Bi. This isn't a major concern presently since the alpha
# emission threshold for 208Bi is negative, i.e. all data is treated as though
# above the unbound threshold, for which discrete state B(GT) values do not apply.
# The B(GT) values were multiplied by g_A^2 = (1.26)^2 to account for their differing
# definition of B(GT), which did not include that weak axial-vector coupling constant.
#
# The B(F) value was taken as the sum-rule value g_V^2*(N_i - Z_i) with g_V=1,
# located at the isobaric analog state
# 
# Fermi transition: Fujita 2013
# https://journals.aps.org/prc/pdf/10.1103/PhysRevC.88.054329, table 1
# E_x = 3.599 MeV with 72%
# E_x = 3.527 MeV with 28%
# B(F) = N - Z = 30 - 26 = 4
# 4 * 0.28 = 1.12
# 4 * 0.72 = 2.88
#
# 3.599 shifted to 3.592 to align with MARLEY
#
# For B(GT): Rapaport 2013, https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0375947483906322
# Following MARLEY convention, g_A^2 = 1.26^2 is multiplied to the B(GT) values
# 
#
#
# Particle IDs and final ion charge
0 1000260560 0
#
1.7     0.365   1
2.7     1.191   1
#3.5    0.540   1
3.527   4       0
#3.592   2.88    0
